{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-216623",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,m,n\\in Z,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all integer $m$ and $n,$ we have $m\\le n.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $0\\le n$ is not true for some integer $n.$ Say, for $n=-1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The predicate\n\n$$\nP(m):\\,∀\\,n\\in Z,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nis false for every integer number $m.$ For example, \n\n$$\nP(-1):\\,∀\\,n\\in Z,\\,-1\\le n\n$$\n\nis false since there are integers less than $-1,$ for example $n=-2.$  Similarly,\n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∀\\,n\\in Z,\\,1\\le n\n$$\n\nis false since we can take $n=0.$ In general, it's sufficient to take $n=m-1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,m,n\\in Z,\\,m\\le n\\,)\n$$\n\n \ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀m\\in Z\\,(\\,{{∀n\\in Z,\\,m\\le n}_{⏟}}_{P(m)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(m)$ is true over the universal set $Z.$ However, according to statement II, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
